PYTHIA 8.318 global recoil for QED matching

The accompanying patch fixes three missing colourless hard-system cases in
SimpleTimeShower.cc. A hard event containing only colourless particles has no
entries in hardPartons. Stock 8.318 then fails to mark it as a hard shower
system and omits its outgoing particles from the global recoil sum. The
patch recognizes colourless hard-production particles when preparing the
system and when constructing the trial and accepted recoil momenta.

Target: https://gitlab.com/Pythia8/releases/-/blob/pythia8318/src/SimpleTimeShower.cc
Original source SHA256:
f20a26c9d6a52ea2330288b9558140cc70abb88cd5158c249c9221417120e49b

Apply in a separate PYTHIA 8.318 source checkout with:
  patch -p1 < /path/to/pythia8318-global-qed.patch
Build that checkout normally and select its library when compiling the
shower driver. This repository does not modify an installed PYTHIA library
or substitute symbols into production shower executables.

The QED global-recoil configuration for a fixed external multiplicity is:
  TimeShower:globalRecoil = on
  TimeShower:globalRecoilMode = 0
  TimeShower:nMaxGlobalRecoil = N
  TimeShower:pTmaxMatch = 1
  TimeShower:pTmaxFudge = 1.
  TimeShower:limitPTmaxGlobal = on
  TimeShower:MEcorrections = off
  TimeShower:weightGluonToQuark = 1
  SpaceShower:pTmaxMatch = 1
  SpaceShower:pTmaxFudge = 1.
  SpaceShower:MEcorrections = off
  SpaceShower:rapidityOrder = off

Here N is the complete Born final-state multiplicity, including leptons,
photons and neutral spectators. With mode 0, this permits global recoil for
the first emission in an S event. An H event has N+1 outgoing particles and
uses local recoil; so does an S event after an ISR or FSR emission. This
selection follows event multiplicity. Mode 2 instead uses proposal and
coloured-parton counters, correcting the proposal count for some rejected
branchings. The modes can differ in shower competition and veto handling;
they are not simply "first accepted" versus "first trial" prescriptions.
The standard QCD mode-2 configuration in the launch script is unchanged.
Modes 1 and 2 apply their special ancestry/counting rules only to coloured
radiators in this PYTHIA release and must not be reused for QED matching.

This configuration applies to a two-incoming hard process with stable
external particles and a fixed Born multiplicity. Decay showers, changed
resonance assignments, and samples with several Born multiplicities require
separate recoil and S/H classification validation. In particular, do not
infer N from the light-QCD npNLO attribute or nPartonsInBorn.

The photon-conversion mass bound is controlled by #Pythia8MMaxGamma in
Cards/FKS_params.dat (default 10 GeV). The launch script passes the same
value to TimeShower:mMaxGamma. Reintegrate when changing it; overriding
TimeShower:mMaxGamma later in pythia8_options would break that agreement.
The MC subtraction retains its infrared limits and does not cut them off
at the shower's nonperturbative termination scale.

Initial-state QED support also follows SimpleSpaceShower 8.318. Its
backward photon-mother -> fermion-daughter conversion exists only for a
physical photon beam, which this launch script does not configure. A
photon PDF inside a proton does not enable that channel. Backward evolution
of a Born incoming photon is supported only with quark mothers (flavours
1 through 5) in hadron beams, not lepton mothers or lepton beams. The raw MC
subtraction is zero for these absent shower channels; the existing G
replacement remains in place. Ordinary charged-fermion photon radiation
remains supported. Both UPC beam codes equal to 2 disable all ISR, as in
the launch script; a single such beam does not disable the other side.

The NLO QED user-interface guard remains enabled. This source patch and the
scalar kernel/support checks alone do not establish an end-to-end NLO QED
S/H cancellation or the normalization of the shower's first-order expansion.
In particular, zero raw subtraction for the absent ISR conversion channels
reproduces the shower's support; it does not provide a finite exclusive
MC@NLO H contribution in their collinear limit. Generated MC/ME limit tests
for those channels fail. Processes containing them remain unsupported until
their matching treatment is implemented; retain the launch guard.

Validation against the actual shower

From the MG5 source root, with an unmodified PYTHIA 8.318 shared-library
build and g++ available, run:
  python3 docs/audits/check_pythia8_qed_global.py /path/to/pythia8318
The script verifies the reference source hash, patches a temporary copy,
and compiles tests/input_files/check_pythia8_qed_global.cc. Only the test
executable overrides the stock SimpleTimeShower definitions; all temporary
files are removed and the installed source and library remain unchanged.

The fixture showers 500 e+ e- -> mu+ mu- tau+ tau- S events and 500 H events
with an extra photon, with massless stable leptons and ISR disabled. A
UserHooks check distinguishes the first global recoil (all three Born
spectators copied) from local recoil (one spectator copied), checks later
emissions remain local, and verifies final-state energy and momentum.
At seed 734612 the unpatched control gave 345 first-emission recoil
failures. The patched shower gave 322 first and 184 later S emissions,
330 first and 206 later H emissions, with zero recoil or event failures.
The largest component-sum momentum residual was 1.85e-13 GeV. This verifies
the recoil configuration and source patch; it does not test matched weights.

Massive W and SUSY radiators

The PYTHIA8 MC counterterms now use the no-MEC SimpleTimeShower numerator
1+z^2 for W-to-W-photon and squark-to-squark-gluon radiation, replacing
the scalar numerator 2z. Final-state W bosons are included in the QED
recoil and shower-scale routing. Incoming W evolution is unsupported.
Other showers retain their existing spin-dependent SUSY kernels.

With MECs off, PYTHIA 8.318 assigns gluinos two ordinary triplet colour
ends, each with CF, rather than CA/2. The raw PYTHIA8 gluino counterterm
now reproduces that behavior. The physical SUSY splitting functions and
the physical soft term supplied by the G replacement are unchanged.
Matching the shower's 2 CF sum does not change it into the physical CA;
full differential matching validation remains necessary.
Soft finiteness relies on the retained G replacement: schematically the
counterterm is G_soft D_PYTHIA + (1-G_soft) C_FKS, with G_soft -> 0 in the
soft limit. C_FKS keeps the physical octet colour charge. Using the raw
2 CF shower kernel alone in R-D would leave a soft mismatch. The G repair
preserves local finiteness but does not repair PYTHIA's soft radiation;
it leaves the usual soft-region G dependence for infrared-safe observables
(see arXiv:1405.0301, eq. 2.117; that paper uses the complementary G).

Run the reproducible massive-radiator shower probe with:
  python3 docs/audits/check_pythia8_massive_radiators.py /path/to/pythia8318
It uses the same isolated-source procedure, with MECs off and global
recoil on (mode 0 for W pairs, mode 2 for coloured pairs). At seed 734612,
all 1500 events passed: W radiation gave 475 checked QED trials and 382
accepted emissions; squarks gave 4929 QCD trials/635 emissions and 130
QED trials/39 emissions; gluinos gave 15292 QCD trials/1231 emissions.
Both charge/colour ends, 1+z^2 numerators and per-end charge/colour factors
were checked. The squark QED probe checks PYTHIA itself; MG5's QED routing
still excludes arbitrary BSM resonances. These are kernel and routing
checks, not a validation of complete matched event weights.

The focused MG5 kernel, mapping, recoil, scale and support suite passed
46 tests. A fresh e+ e- -> W+ W- [QED] export also built the production
soft-limit executable and passed 20 random soft tests for each W emitter
(40/40), including the existing G replacement.
